1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,877
One question is never enough.

2
00:00:02,197 --> 00:00:07,296
Real analysts ask many, adaptively,
each chosen after seeing earlier answers.

3
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:10,325
Maybe you spot a spike in one bin,
and zoom in.

4
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:12,572
The paper's Theorem 1 handles this.

5
00:00:12,892 --> 00:00:14,112
Write the probability

6
00:00:14,112 --> 00:00:17,074
of the whole transcript as a product,
step by step.

7
00:00:17,394 --> 00:00:21,700
The analyst's choice of the next question
is the same in both worlds,

8
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:25,756
so in the ratio it cancels,
leaving one Laplace ratio per answer.

9
00:00:26,076 --> 00:00:30,640
If you know reinforcement learning,
this is the trajectory-ratio trick:

10
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:33,569
whatever is identical
in both worlds cancels,

11
00:00:33,569 --> 00:00:35,847
and the per-step log ratios add up.

12
00:00:36,083 --> 00:00:39,966
So privacy losses add up,
and epsilon behaves like a budget.

13
00:00:40,286 --> 00:00:44,232
Each answer spends part of it;
once it is spent, the curator stops.

14
00:00:44,552 --> 00:00:46,927
That is the answer to Dinur and Nissim:

15
00:00:46,927 --> 00:00:50,825
the budget makes the limit on questions
explicit and measurable.

16
00:00:52,367 --> 00:00:54,778
You can now compute a real saving yourself.

17
00:00:55,098 --> 00:00:58,724
Pause: a histogram with d bins,
total budget epsilon.

18
00:00:59,044 --> 00:01:03,140
Treat each bin as its own counting query
and split the budget evenly.

19
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:05,337
How much noise does each bin get?

20
00:01:18,183 --> 00:01:22,855
Each bin gets one over d of the budget,
so noise of scale d over epsilon.

21
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:25,985
Treated as one query with sensitivity two,

22
00:01:25,985 --> 00:01:29,063
each bin gets two over epsilon,
whatever d is.

23
00:01:29,383 --> 00:01:34,230
The earlier framework's sharper analysis
got this down to about the square root of d,

24
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:35,655
but it still grew with d.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,368
Sensitivity reaches far beyond counts.

26
00:01:39,688 --> 00:01:41,459
Three examples from the paper.

27
00:01:41,767 --> 00:01:43,727
First, picture a social network

28
00:01:43,727 --> 00:01:47,015
where each possible link
is one row of the database.

29
00:01:47,335 --> 00:01:50,194
How many links must you cut
to split the network in two?

30
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:54,246
That minimum cut moves by at most one
when one link changes,

31
00:01:54,246 --> 00:01:55,676
so it is one-sensitive.

32
00:01:55,996 --> 00:01:58,075
In general, any question of the form,

33
00:01:58,075 --> 00:02:02,737
how many rows would you have to change
to make something true, has sensitivity one.

34
00:02:02,983 --> 00:02:07,250
Second: if an algorithm
that rarely looks at any particular row,

35
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:10,183
like one working from a small random sample,

36
00:02:10,183 --> 00:02:13,383
approximates f
to within sigma most of the time,

37
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:17,383
on every database,
then f has sensitivity at most two sigma.

38
00:02:17,703 --> 00:02:18,791
That is Lemma 1.

39
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:21,386
Third, the answer need not be a number:

40
00:02:21,386 --> 00:02:26,030
a ranking, a set, a string of bits,
anything with a distance between answers.

41
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:29,863
Pick an output with probability
that decays exponentially

42
00:02:29,863 --> 00:02:32,267
with its distance from the true answer,

43
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:35,225
at a rate of epsilon
over twice the sensitivity.

44
00:02:35,545 --> 00:02:40,207
For bit strings, that flips each bit
with probability a little below one half:

45
00:02:40,207 --> 00:02:42,777
Warner's coin again, applied to the answer.

46
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:47,345
Now the last part of the paper.

47
00:02:47,665 --> 00:02:52,604
Statisticians and data miners traditionally
prefer the non-interactive model:

48
00:02:52,604 --> 00:02:56,966
sanitize the data once, publish it,
and let anyone compute anything.

49
00:02:57,286 --> 00:02:59,142
Can that work under this definition?

50
00:02:59,384 --> 00:03:01,261
The paper proves a striking limit.

51
00:03:01,581 --> 00:03:03,501
Let each row be a string of d bits.

52
00:03:03,821 --> 00:03:06,637
Here is a family of simple counting queries:

53
00:03:06,637 --> 00:03:10,093
give each row its own mask,
a subset of bit positions,

54
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:14,189
and count the rows with an odd number
of ones inside their mask.

55
00:03:14,509 --> 00:03:18,106
Each query has sensitivity one,
so an interactive curator

56
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:22,146
can answer any one of them
with noise of about one over epsilon.

57
00:03:22,384 --> 00:03:25,669
But a one-shot release must prepare
for all of them at once.

58
00:03:25,989 --> 00:03:29,773
Theorem 3 shows that for at least two thirds
of these queries,

59
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:33,130
the release looks almost the same
for a random database

60
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:36,182
where every row has even parity,
true answer zero,

61
00:03:36,182 --> 00:03:39,173
as for one
where every row is odd, true answer n.

62
00:03:39,493 --> 00:03:43,237
The most extreme difference possible,
and it cannot be seen,

63
00:03:43,237 --> 00:03:45,921
unless the database is exponentially large:

64
00:03:45,921 --> 00:03:50,352
roughly, every four extra bits per row
doubles the rows you would need.

65
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,868
Pause and ponder: couldn't an analyst

66
00:03:52,868 --> 00:03:56,424
just ask the interactive curator
all of these queries too?

67
00:04:07,267 --> 00:04:08,973
No: the budget would run out.

68
00:04:09,293 --> 00:04:11,152
The curator only has to answer

69
00:04:11,152 --> 00:04:14,499
the few questions someone actually asks,
chosen later.

70
00:04:14,819 --> 00:04:17,781
A published release cannot know
which few those will be,

71
00:04:17,781 --> 00:04:20,109
so it must be ready for all of them at once.

72
00:04:20,429 --> 00:04:21,688
That is where it breaks.

73
00:04:21,934 --> 00:04:23,811
The proof idea uses two facts.

74
00:04:24,131 --> 00:04:28,526
Privacy forces the release
to treat every possible row almost alike.

75
00:04:28,846 --> 00:04:33,407
And a random mask splits all rows
into two halves mixed like salt and pepper,

76
00:04:33,407 --> 00:04:36,547
so the even half
is like a random poll of everything.

77
00:04:36,867 --> 00:04:40,110
Then the chain trick:
start from completely random rows,

78
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:42,542
and swap them one at a time for even ones.

79
00:04:42,862 --> 00:04:46,381
Each swap barely moves the output,
and unless n is huge,

80
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:48,643
all n swaps together barely move it.

81
00:04:48,963 --> 00:04:53,315
The same goes for odd rows,
so both databases look like random rows.

82
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:55,819
Warner's coin, randomized response,

83
00:04:55,819 --> 00:05:00,423
where each person scrambles their own row
so nobody holds the raw data,

84
00:05:00,423 --> 00:05:01,784
is even more limited.

85
00:05:02,104 --> 00:05:06,046
Proposition 2:
even when every row uses the same mask,

86
00:05:06,046 --> 00:05:09,476
for most masks
it cannot estimate the odd count

87
00:05:09,476 --> 00:05:11,812
unless n is exponentially large.

88
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:13,628
Careful with the quantifiers.

89
00:05:13,948 --> 00:05:18,258
For any one query known in advance,
a one-shot release can answer it well.

90
00:05:18,578 --> 00:05:22,478
What is impossible,
unless the database is exponentially large,

91
00:05:22,478 --> 00:05:25,511
is one private release
that answers most of them.

92
00:05:25,831 --> 00:05:29,677
The lesson: for broad, flexible accuracy
with strong privacy,

93
00:05:29,677 --> 00:05:31,506
keep the curator in the loop.

94
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:35,689
Back to our map,
to see what grew from this paper.

95
00:05:36,009 --> 00:05:40,233
The same year, in an invited paper
titled simply Differential Privacy,

96
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:43,070
Dwork introduced the name
the field still uses.

97
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:48,229
Another 2006 paper,
with Kenthapadi, McSherry, Mironov and Naor,

98
00:05:48,229 --> 00:05:50,195
added a tiny slack, delta.

99
00:05:50,515 --> 00:05:54,035
Remember the Gaussian
whose ratio escaped the band in the tails?

100
00:05:54,355 --> 00:05:58,131
Delta pays for those rare tails,
and lets the bell curve back in.

101
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:03,615
Scoring every possible answer became
McSherry and Talwar's exponential mechanism.

102
00:06:03,935 --> 00:06:07,489
The privacy budget grew
into a theory of composition,

103
00:06:07,489 --> 00:06:10,754
with a surprise
from Dwork, Rothblum and Vadhan:

104
00:06:10,754 --> 00:06:12,251
allow that tiny delta,

105
00:06:12,251 --> 00:06:17,012
and the total loss of k questions
grows only like the square root of k.

106
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:21,253
Warner's coin, the model
Section four showed is most limited,

107
00:06:21,253 --> 00:06:24,338
is what we now call
local differential privacy,

108
00:06:24,338 --> 00:06:27,554
used by Google in Chrome
and by Apple on iPhones.

109
00:06:27,874 --> 00:06:29,197
Why the weakest model?

110
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:33,865
Nobody has to be trusted with the raw data,
and with millions of users,

111
00:06:33,865 --> 00:06:36,621
a few simple statistics
can afford the noise.

112
00:06:36,867 --> 00:06:40,004
In 2016,
Abadi and colleagues made it practical

113
00:06:40,004 --> 00:06:44,209
to train deep networks privately:
clip each example's gradient,

114
00:06:44,209 --> 00:06:47,547
like our income cap,
which bounds its sensitivity;

115
00:06:47,547 --> 00:06:48,835
add Gaussian noise;

116
00:06:48,835 --> 00:06:51,886
and track the budget
over thousands of steps.

117
00:06:52,206 --> 00:06:55,747
If you train models on patient data,
this is your entry point.

118
00:06:56,067 --> 00:06:59,690
A trained network is just another f of x,
and attacks that ask

119
00:06:59,690 --> 00:07:04,365
whether a patient was in the training set
are our subtraction attack, scaled up.

120
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:07,738
And for the 2020 census,
the US Census Bureau

121
00:07:07,738 --> 00:07:11,714
protected its published tables
with differential privacy;

122
00:07:11,714 --> 00:07:15,480
only a few counts,
like state populations, were exact.

123
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:17,485
But that is a one-shot release.

124
00:07:17,805 --> 00:07:20,429
Pause and ponder:
does Section four forbid it?

125
00:07:29,267 --> 00:07:34,430
No. The Census tuned its release
for a fixed set of tables chosen in advance.

126
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:37,237
Section four only rules out one release

127
00:07:37,237 --> 00:07:40,808
that is accurate
for most of a huge family of questions.

128
00:07:44,150 --> 00:07:48,721
Let's recap. One:
privacy means changing any one person's row

129
00:07:48,721 --> 00:07:54,070
changes the probability of any output
by at most a factor of e to the epsilon.

130
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:58,635
Two: a query's sensitivity
is the most one row can change its answer.

131
00:07:58,955 --> 00:08:02,441
Three: Laplace noise
with scale sensitivity over epsilon

132
00:08:02,441 --> 00:08:06,550
makes it private, with error
that does not grow with the database.

133
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:11,650
Four: one private published table
cannot answer most simple parity counts

134
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:14,465
unless the database is exponentially large.

135
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,108
Some questions to test yourself;
pause after each.

136
00:08:18,428 --> 00:08:20,234
First: what is the sensitivity

137
00:08:20,234 --> 00:08:23,185
of the average of n numbers
between zero and one?

138
00:08:31,433 --> 00:08:36,284
Second: in a medical imaging dataset,
each patient contributes fifty slices,

139
00:08:36,284 --> 00:08:39,284
and each slice's gradient
is clipped to size C.

140
00:08:39,604 --> 00:08:42,591
How much can one patient
change the summed gradient?

141
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:56,225
Third: why does the median give
the Laplace mechanism trouble?

142
00:08:56,545 --> 00:08:59,959
Answers, and more exercises,
are in the companion notes.

143
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,103
Next time someone says a dataset is safe
because it is anonymized,

144
00:09:12,103 --> 00:09:14,173
you will know the better questions:

145
00:09:14,173 --> 00:09:17,544
what is the epsilon,
and what counts as one person's row?

